<P><B>Fortran</B> (formerly <B>FORTRAN</B>, derived from <B>Formula =
Translating=20
System</B>) is a general-purpose, <A title=3D"Imperative programming" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Imperative_programming">imperative<=
/A>=20
<A title=3D"Programming language" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Programming_language">programming=20
language</A> that is especially suited to <A title=3D"Numerical =
analysis" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Numerical_analysis">numeric=20
computation</A> and <A title=3D"Computational science" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computational_science">scientific=20
computing</A>. Originally developed by <A title=3D"IBM" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IBM">IBM</A><SUP=20
class=3D"reference" id=3D"cite_ref-Where_1-0"><A =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran#cite_note-Where-1"><SPAN>[<=
/SPAN>1<SPAN>]</SPAN></A></SUP>=20
in the 1950s for scientific and engineering applications, Fortran came =
to=20
dominate this area of programming early on and has been in continuous =
use for=20
over half a century in computationally intensive areas such as <A =
title=3D"Numerical weather prediction"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Numerical_weather_prediction">numer=
ical=20
weather prediction</A>, <A title=3D"Finite element method" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Finite_element_method">finite=20
element analysis</A>, <A title=3D"Computational fluid dynamics" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computational_fluid_dynamics">compu=
tational=20
fluid dynamics</A>, <A title=3D"Computational physics" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computational_physics">computationa=
l=20
physics</A> and <A title=3D"Computational chemistry" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computational_chemistry">computatio=
nal=20
chemistry</A>. It is a popular language for <A title=3D"High-performance =
computing"=20
class=3D"mw-redirect" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/High-performance_computing">high-pe=
rformance=20
computing</A><SUP class=3D"reference" id=3D"cite_ref-hpc_2-0"><A =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran#cite_note-hpc-2"><SPAN>[</S=
PAN>2<SPAN>]</SPAN></A></SUP>=20
and is used for programs that benchmark and rank the world's <A =
title=3D"TOP500"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/TOP500">fastest =
supercomputers</A>.<SUP=20
class=3D"reference" id=3D"cite_ref-3"><A =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran#cite_note-3"><SPAN>[</SPAN>=
3<SPAN>]</SPAN></A></SUP></P>
<P>Fortran encompasses a lineage of versions, each of which evolved to =
add=20
extensions to the language while usually retaining compatibility with =
prior=20
versions. Successive versions have added support for <A =
title=3D"Structured programming"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Structured_programming">structured =

programming</A> and processing of character-based data (<A =
title=3D"FORTRAN 77"=20
class=3D"mw-redirect" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FORTRAN_77">FORTRAN=20
77</A>), <A title=3D"Array programming" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Array_programming">array=20
programming</A>, <A title=3D"Modular programming" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Modular_programming">modular=20
programming</A> and <A title=3D"Generic programming" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Generic_programming">generic=20
programming</A> (<A title=3D"Fortran 90" class=3D"mw-redirect" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran_90">Fortran=20
90</A>), <A title=3D"High Performance Fortran" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/High_Performance_Fortran">high=20
performance Fortran</A> (<A title=3D"Fortran 95" class=3D"mw-redirect" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran_95">Fortran=20
95</A>), <A title=3D"Object-oriented programming" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Object-oriented_programming">object=
-oriented=20
programming</A> (<A title=3D"Fortran 2003" class=3D"mw-redirect" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran_2003">Fortran=20
2003</A>) and <A title=3D"Concurrent programming" class=3D"mw-redirect" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Concurrent_programming">concurrent =

programming</A> (<A title=3D"Fortran 2008" class=3D"mw-redirect" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran_2008">Fortran=20
2008</A>).</P>
