<H2><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" id=3D"Naming">Naming</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A title=3D"Edit section: =
Naming" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran&amp;action=3D=
edit&amp;section=3D1">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H2>
<P>The names of earlier versions of the language through FORTRAN 77 were =

conventionally spelled in all-caps (FORTRAN 77 was the last version in =
which the=20
use of lowercase letters in keywords was strictly non-standard). The=20
capitalization has been dropped in referring to newer versions beginning =
with=20
Fortran 90. The official language <A title=3D"International standard" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_standard">standards</=
A>=20
now refer to the language as "Fortran".</P>
<P>Because the capitalization usage was sometimes inconsistent, this =
article=20
adopts the convention of using the all-caps <I>FORTRAN</I> in referring =
to=20
versions of the language up to FORTRAN 77 and the title-caps =
<I>Fortran</I> in=20
referring to versions of the language from Fortran 90 onward. This =
convention is=20
reflected in the capitalization of <I>FORTRAN</I> in the <A =
title=3D"American National Standards Institute"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_National_Standards_Institu=
te">American=20
National Standards Institute</A> (ANSI) X3.9-1966 (FORTRAN 66) and ANSI=20
X3.9-1978 (FORTRAN 77) standards and the title caps <I>Fortran</I> in =
the ANSI=20
X3.198-1992 (Fortran 90), ISO/IEC 1539-1:1997 (Fortran 95) and ISO/IEC=20
1539-1:2004 (Fortran 2003) standards.</P>
<H2><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" id=3D"History">History</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A title=3D"Edit section: =
History" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran&amp;action=3D=
edit&amp;section=3D2">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H2>
<DIV class=3D"thumb tright">
<DIV class=3D"thumbinner" style=3D"width: 322px;"><A class=3D"image" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:IBM_704_mainframe.gif"><IMG=20
width=3D"320" height=3D"210" class=3D"thumbimage" alt=3D"" =
src=3D"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/IBM_704_=
mainframe.gif/320px-IBM_704_mainframe.gif"=20
data-file-height=3D"233" data-file-width=3D"355" =
srcset=3D"//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7d/IBM_704_mainframe=
.gif 1.5x, =
//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7d/IBM_704_mainframe.gif =
2x"></A>=20
<DIV class=3D"thumbcaption">
<DIV class=3D"magnify"><A title=3D"Enlarge" class=3D"internal" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:IBM_704_mainframe.gif"></A></D=
IV>
An <A title=3D"IBM 704" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IBM_704">IBM 704</A>=20
<A title=3D"Mainframe computer" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mainframe_computer">mainframe=20
computer</A></DIV></DIV></DIV>
<P>In late 1953, <A title=3D"John Backus" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Backus">John=20
W. Backus</A> submitted a proposal to his superiors at <A title=3D"IBM" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IBM">IBM</A>=20
to develop a more practical alternative to <A title=3D"Assembly =
language" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Assembly_language">assembly=20
language</A> for programming their <A title=3D"IBM 704" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IBM_704">IBM=20
704</A> mainframe computer. Backus' historic FORTRAN team consisted of=20
programmers Richard Goldberg, Sheldon F. Best, Harlan Herrick, Peter =
Sheridan,=20
<A title=3D"Roy Nutt" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roy_Nutt">Roy Nutt</A>,=20
Robert Nelson, Irving Ziller, <A title=3D"Lois Haibt" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lois_Haibt">Lois=20
Haibt</A>, and <A title=3D"David Sayre" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_Sayre">David=20
Sayre</A>.<SUP class=3D"reference" id=3D"cite_ref-4"><A =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran#cite_note-4"><SPAN>[</SPAN>=
4<SPAN>]</SPAN></A></SUP>=20
Its concepts included easier entry of equations into a computer, an idea =

developed by <A title=3D"J. Halcombe Laning" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/J._Halcombe_Laning">J.=20
Halcombe Laning</A> and demonstrated in his <A title=3D"Laning and =
Zierler system"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laning_and_Zierler_system">GEORGE=20
compiler</A> of 1952.<SUP class=3D"reference" id=3D"cite_ref-5"><A =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran#cite_note-5"><SPAN>[</SPAN>=
5<SPAN>]</SPAN></A></SUP></P>
<P>A draft specification for <I>The IBM Mathematical Formula Translating =

System</I> was completed by mid-1954. The first manual for FORTRAN =
appeared in=20
October 1956, with the first FORTRAN <A title=3D"Compiler" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Compiler">compiler</A>=20
delivered in April 1957. This was the first <A title=3D"Optimizing =
compiler" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Optimizing_compiler">optimizing=20
compiler</A>, because customers were reluctant to use a <A =
title=3D"High-level programming language"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/High-level_programming_language">hi=
gh-level=20
programming language</A> unless its compiler could generate code which=20
performance was comparable to that of hand-coded <A title=3D"Assembly =
language"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Assembly_language">assembly=20
language</A>.<SUP class=3D"reference" id=3D"cite_ref-6"><A =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran#cite_note-6"><SPAN>[</SPAN>=
6<SPAN>]</SPAN></A></SUP></P>
<P>While the community was skeptical that this new method could possibly =

outperform hand-coding, it reduced the number of programming <A =
title=3D"Statement (programming)"=20
class=3D"mw-redirect"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Statement_(programming)">statements=
</A>=20
necessary to operate a machine by a factor of 20, and quickly gained =
acceptance.=20
John Backus said during a 1979 interview with <I>Think</I>, the IBM =
employee=20
magazine, "Much of my work has come from being lazy. I didn't like =
writing=20
programs, and so, when I was working on the <A title=3D"IBM 701" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IBM_701">IBM=20
701</A>, writing programs for computing missile trajectories, I started =
work on=20
a programming system to make it easier to write programs."<SUP =
class=3D"reference"=20
id=3D"cite_ref-7"><A=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran#cite_note-7"><SPAN>[</SPAN>=
7<SPAN>]</SPAN></A></SUP></P>
<P>The language was widely adopted by scientists for writing numerically =

intensive programs, which encouraged compiler writers to produce =
compilers that=20
could generate faster and more efficient code. The inclusion of a <A =
title=3D"Complex data type"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Complex_data_type">complex number =
data=20
type</A> in the language made Fortran especially suited to technical=20
applications such as electrical engineering. In 1957 USAF Lt. Edward =
Fredkin=20
used the new Fortran, on the Lincoln Laboratory IBM 704 computer, to =
test the=20
first implementation of his <A title=3D"Trie" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trie">Trie</A>=20
Memory system (Trie Memory, Communications of the ACM,1962). Trie Memory =
has=20
since become today's predominant information storage and retrieval =
methodology.=20
By 1960, versions of FORTRAN were available for the <A title=3D"IBM 709" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IBM_709">IBM=20
709</A>, <A title=3D"IBM 650"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IBM_650">650</A>, <A title=3D"IBM =
1620" href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IBM_1620">1620</A>,=20
and <A title=3D"IBM 7090" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IBM_7090">7090</A>=20
computers. Significantly, the increasing popularity of FORTRAN spurred =
competing=20
computer manufacturers to provide FORTRAN compilers for their machines, =
so that=20
by 1963 over 40 FORTRAN compilers existed. For these reasons, FORTRAN is =

considered to be the first widely used programming language supported =
across a=20
variety of computer architectures.</P>
<P>The development of FORTRAN paralleled the <A title=3D"History of =
compiler writing"=20
class=3D"mw-redirect" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_compiler_writing">early =

evolution of compiler technology</A>, and many advances in the theory =
and design=20
of <A title=3D"Compiler"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Compiler">compilers</A> were =
specifically=20
motivated by the need to generate efficient code for FORTRAN =
programs.</P>
<H3><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" id=3D"FORTRAN">FORTRAN</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A title=3D"Edit section: =
FORTRAN" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran&amp;action=3D=
edit&amp;section=3D3">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H3>
<P>The initial release of FORTRAN for the IBM 704 contained 32 <A =
title=3D"Statement (programming)"=20
class=3D"mw-redirect"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Statement_(programming)">statements=
</A>,=20
including:</P>
<UL>
  <LI><CODE>DIMENSION</CODE> and <CODE>EQUIVALENCE</CODE> =
statements</LI>
  <LI>Assignment statements</LI>
  <LI>Three-way <A title=3D"Arithmetic IF" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arithmetic_IF"><I>arithmetic</I>=20
  <CODE>IF</CODE></A> statement, which passed control to one of three =
locations=20
  in the program depending on whether the result of the arithmetic =
statement was=20
  negative, zero, or positive</LI>
  <LI><CODE>IF</CODE> statements for checking exceptions =
(<CODE>ACCUMULATOR=20
  OVERFLOW</CODE>, <CODE>QUOTIENT OVERFLOW</CODE>, and <CODE>DIVIDE=20
  CHECK</CODE>); and <CODE>IF</CODE> statements for manipulating <A =
title=3D"Front panel"=20
  href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Front_panel">sense switches and =
sense=20
  lights</A></LI>
  <LI><CODE>GOTO</CODE>, computed <CODE>GOTO</CODE>, =
<CODE>ASSIGN</CODE>, and=20
  assigned <CODE>GOTO</CODE></LI>
  <LI><CODE>DO</CODE> loops</LI>
  <LI>Formatted I/O: <CODE>FORMAT</CODE>, <CODE>READ</CODE>, <CODE>READ =
INPUT=20
  TAPE</CODE>, <CODE>WRITE</CODE>, <CODE>WRITE OUTPUT TAPE</CODE>,=20
  <CODE>PRINT</CODE>, and <CODE>PUNCH</CODE></LI>
  <LI>Unformatted I/O: <CODE>READ TAPE</CODE>, <CODE>READ DRUM</CODE>,=20
  <CODE>WRITE TAPE</CODE>, and <CODE>WRITE DRUM</CODE></LI>
  <LI>Other I/O: <CODE>END FILE</CODE>, <CODE>REWIND</CODE>, and=20
  <CODE>BACKSPACE</CODE></LI>
  <LI><CODE>PAUSE</CODE>, <CODE>STOP</CODE>, and =
<CODE>CONTINUE</CODE></LI>
  <LI><CODE>FREQUENCY</CODE> statement (for providing <A =
title=3D"Optimization (computer science)"=20
  class=3D"mw-redirect" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Optimization_(computer_science)">op=
timization</A>=20
  hints to the compiler).</LI></UL>
<P>The arithmetic <CODE>IF</CODE> statement was similar to a three-way =
branch=20
instruction on the IBM 704. However, the 704 branch instructions all =
contained=20
only one destination address (e.g., TZE =E2=80=94 Transfer AC Zero, TNZ =
=E2=80=94 Transfer AC=20
Not Zero, TPL =E2=80=94 Transfer AC Plus, TMI =E2=80=94 Transfer AC =
Minus). The machine (and its=20
successors in the <A title=3D"IBM 700/7000 series" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IBM_700/7000_series#Scientific_Arch=
itecture_.28704.2F709.2F7090.2F7094.29">700/7000=20
series</A>) did have a three-way <I>skip</I> instruction (CAS =E2=80=94 =
Compare AC with=20
Storage), but using this instruction to implement the <CODE>IF</CODE> =
would=20
consume 4 instruction words, require the constant Zero in a word of =
storage, and=20
take 3 machine cycles to execute; using the Transfer instructions to =
implement=20
the <CODE>IF</CODE> could be done in 1 to 3 instruction words, required =
no=20
constants in storage, and take 1 to 3 machine cycles to execute. An =
optimizing=20
compiler like FORTRAN would most likely select the more compact and =
usually=20
faster Transfers instead of the Compare (use of Transfers also allowed =
the=20
<CODE>FREQUENCY</CODE> statement to optimize <CODE>IF</CODE>s, which =
could not=20
be done using the Compare). Also the Compare considered =E2=88=920 and =
+0 to be=20
different values while the Transfer Zero and Transfer Not Zero =
considered them=20
to be the same.</P>
<P>The <CODE>FREQUENCY</CODE> statement in FORTRAN was used originally =
(and=20
optionally) to give branch probabilities for the three branch cases of =
the=20
arithmetic IF statement. The first FORTRAN compiler used this weighting =
to=20
perform <I>at compile time</I> a <A title=3D"Monte Carlo method" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monte_Carlo_method">Monte=20
Carlo simulation</A> of the generated code, the results of which were =
used to=20
optimize the placement of basic blocks in memory =E2=80=94 a very =
sophisticated=20
optimization for its time. The Monte Carlo technique is documented in =
Backus et=20
al.'s paper on this original implementation, <I>The FORTRAN Automatic =
Coding=20
System</I>:</P>
<BLOCKQUOTE>
  <P>The fundamental unit of program is the <A title=3D"Basic block" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basic_block">basic=20
  block</A>; a basic block is a stretch of program which has one entry =
point and=20
  one exit point. The purpose of section 4 is to prepare for section 5 a =
table=20
  of predecessors (PRED table) which enumerates the basic blocks and =
lists for=20
  every basic block each of the basic blocks which can be its immediate=20
  predecessor in flow, together with the absolute frequency of each such =
basic=20
  block link. This table is obtained by running the program once in =
Monte-Carlo=20
  fashion, in which the outcome of conditional transfers arising out of =
IF-type=20
  statements and computed GO TO'S is determined by a random number =
generator=20
  suitably weighted according to whatever FREQUENCY statements have been =

  provided.<SUP class=3D"reference" id=3D"cite_ref-Backus57_8-0"><A =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran#cite_note-Backus57-8"><SPAN=
>[</SPAN>8<SPAN>]</SPAN></A></SUP></P></BLOCKQUOTE>
<P>Many years later, the <CODE>FREQUENCY</CODE> statement had no effect =
on the=20
code, and was treated as a comment statement, since the compilers no =
longer did=20
this kind of compile-time simulation. A similar fate has befallen =
<I>compiler=20
hints</I> in several other programming languages; for example <A =
title=3D"C (programming language)"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C_(programming_language)">C</A>'s=20
<CODE>register</CODE> keyword.<SUP class=3D"noprint Inline-Template =
Template-Fact"=20
style=3D"white-space: nowrap;">[<I><A title=3D"Wikipedia:Citation =
needed" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed"><SPAN=20
title=3D"This claim needs references to reliable sources. (September =
2013)">citation=20
needed</SPAN></A></I>]</SUP></P>
<H4><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" =
id=3D"Fixed_layout_and_punched_cards">Fixed layout=20
and punched cards</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN =
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A=20
title=3D"Edit section: Fixed layout and punched cards" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran&amp;action=3D=
edit&amp;section=3D4">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H4>
<DIV class=3D"thumb tright">
<DIV class=3D"thumbinner" style=3D"width: 222px;"><A class=3D"image" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:FortranCardPROJ039.agr.jpg"><I=
