//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/FortranCardPROJ039.ag=
r.jpg/440px-FortranCardPROJ039.agr.jpg 2x"></A>=20
<DIV class=3D"thumbcaption">
<DIV class=3D"magnify"><A title=3D"Enlarge" class=3D"internal" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:FortranCardPROJ039.agr.jpg"></=
A></DIV>
FORTRAN code on a <A title=3D"Punched card" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Punched_card">punched=20
card</A>, showing the specialized uses of columns 1-5, 6 and=20
73-80.</DIV></DIV></DIV>
<P>Before the development of disk files, text editors and terminals, =
programs=20
were most often entered on a <A title=3D"Keypunch" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Keypunch">keypunch</A>=20
keyboard onto 80 column <A title=3D"Punched card" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Punched_card">punched=20
cards</A>, one line to a card. The resulting deck of cards would be fed =
into a=20
card reader to be compiled. Punched card codes included no lower case =
letters or=20
many special characters, and special versions of the IBM 026 keypunch =
were=20
offered that would correctly print the repurposed special characters =
used in=20
Fortran. <I>See <A title=3D"Computer programming in the punched card =
era" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computer_programming_in_the_punched=
_card_era">Computer=20
programming in the punched card era</A>.</I></P>
<P>Reflecting punched card input practice, Fortran programs were =
originally=20
written in a fixed column format. A letter "C" in column 1 caused the =
entire=20
card to be treated as a comment and ignored by the compiler. Otherwise, =
the card=20
was divided into four fields. Columns 1 to 5 were the label field: a =
sequence of=20
digits here was taken as a label for the purpose of a GOTO or a FORMAT =
reference=20
in a WRITE or READ statement. Column 6 was a continuation field: a =
non-blank=20
character here caused the card to be taken as a continuation of the =
statement on=20
the prior card. Columns 7 to 72 served as the statement field. Columns =
73 to 80=20
were ignored, so they could be used for identification information. One =
such use=20
was punching a sequence number which could be used to re-order cards if =
a stack=20
of cards was dropped, though in practice this was reserved for stable,=20
production programs. An <A title=3D"IBM 519" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IBM_519">IBM=20
519</A> could be used to copy a program deck and add sequence numbers. =
Some=20
early compilers, e.g., the IBM 650's, had additional restrictions due to =

limitations on their card readers.<SUP class=3D"reference" =
id=3D"cite_ref-9"><A=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran#cite_note-9"><SPAN>[</SPAN>=
9<SPAN>]</SPAN></A></SUP>=20
Keypunches could be programmed to tab to column 7 and skip out after =
column 72.=20
Later compilers relaxed most fixed format restrictions and the =
requirement was=20
eliminated in the Fortran 90 standard.</P>
<P>Within the statement field, <A title=3D"Whitespace characters" =
class=3D"mw-redirect"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Whitespace_characters">whitespace=20
characters</A> (blanks) were generally ignored. This allowed omitting =
spaces=20
between tokens for brevity, or including spaces within identifiers for =
clarity.=20
For example, <CODE>AVG OF X</CODE> was a valid identifier, and =
equivalent to=20
<CODE>AVGOFX</CODE>. Character strings, originally allowed only in =
FORMAT=20
statements, were prefixed by a character count and the letter H (e.g., =
26HTHIS=20
IS ALPHANUMERIC DATA.), allowing blanks to be retained in the =
string.</P>
<H3><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" id=3D"FORTRAN_II">FORTRAN II</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A title=3D"Edit section: =
FORTRAN II" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran&amp;action=3D=
edit&amp;section=3D5">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H3>
<P>IBM's <I>FORTRAN II</I> appeared in 1958. The main enhancement was to =
support=20
<A title=3D"Procedural programming" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Procedural_programming">procedural =

programming</A> by allowing user-written subroutines and functions which =

returned values, with parameters passed by <A title=3D"Call by =
reference" class=3D"mw-redirect"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Call_by_reference#Call_by_reference=
">reference</A>.=20
The COMMON statement provided a way for subroutines to access common (or =
<A=20
title=3D"Global variable"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_variable">global</A>) =
variables. Six=20
new statements were introduced:</P>
<UL>
  <LI><CODE>SUBROUTINE</CODE>, <CODE>FUNCTION</CODE>, and =
<CODE>END</CODE></LI>
  <LI><CODE>CALL</CODE> and <CODE>RETURN</CODE></LI>
  <LI><CODE>COMMON</CODE></LI></UL>
<P>Over the next few years, FORTRAN II would also add support for the=20
<CODE>DOUBLE PRECISION</CODE> and <CODE>COMPLEX</CODE> data types.</P>
<P>Early FORTRAN compilers supported no <A title=3D"Recursion (computer =
science)"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Recursion_(computer_science)">recur=
sion</A>=20
in subroutines. Early computer architectures supported no concept of a =
stack,=20
and when they did directly support subroutine calls, the return location =
was=20
often stored in one fixed location adjacent to the subroutine code, =
which does=20
not permit a subroutine to be called again before a prior call of the =
subroutine=20
has returned. Although not specified in Fortran 77, many F77 compilers =
supported=20
recursion as an option, while it became a standard in Fortran 90.<SUP =
class=3D"reference"=20
id=3D"cite_ref-10"><A=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran#cite_note-10"><SPAN>[</SPAN=
>10<SPAN>]</SPAN></A></SUP></P>
<H4><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" id=3D"Simple_FORTRAN_II_program">Simple =
FORTRAN II=20
program</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A title=3D"Edit section: =
