IV>
A FORTRAN coding form, printed on paper and intended to be used by =
programmers=20
to prepare programs for punching onto cards by <A title=3D"Keypunch" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Keypunch">keypunch</A>=20
operators. Now obsolete.</DIV></DIV></DIV>
<P>IBM also developed a <I>FORTRAN III</I> in 1958 that allowed for =
inline=20
assembly code among other features; however, this version was never =
released as=20
a product. Like the 704 FORTRAN and FORTRAN II, FORTRAN III included=20
machine-dependent features that made code written in it unportable from =
machine=20
to machine. Early versions of FORTRAN provided by other vendors suffered =
from=20
the same disadvantage.</P>
<H3><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" id=3D"IBM_1401_FORTRAN">IBM 1401 =
FORTRAN</SPAN><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN =
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A title=3D"Edit section: IBM =
1401 FORTRAN"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran&amp;action=3D=
edit&amp;section=3D8">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H3>
<P>FORTRAN was provided for the IBM 1401 computer by an innovative =
63-phase=20
compiler that ran entirely in its <A title=3D"Magnetic core memory" =
class=3D"mw-redirect"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magnetic_core_memory">core =
memory</A> of=20
only 8000 (6-bit) characters. The compiler could be run from tape, or =
from a=20
2200-card deck; it used no further tape or disk storage. It kept the =
program in=20
memory and loaded <A title=3D"Overlay (programming)" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Overlay_(programming)">overlays</A>=
=20
that gradually transformed it, in place, into executable form, as =
described by=20
Haines.<SUP class=3D"reference" id=3D"cite_ref-11"><A =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran#cite_note-11"><SPAN>[</SPAN=
>11<SPAN>]</SPAN></A></SUP>=20
and in <A class=3D"external text" =
href=3D"http://www.bitsavers.org/pdf/ibm/140x/C24-1455-2_Fortran_Specific=
ations_and_Operating_Procedures_Apr65.pdf"=20
rel=3D"nofollow">IBM document C24-1455</A>. The executable form was not =
entirely=20
<A title=3D"Machine language" class=3D"mw-redirect" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Machine_language">machine=20
language</A>; rather, floating-point arithmetic, subscripting, =
input/output, and=20
function references were interpreted, anticipating <A title=3D"UCSD =
Pascal" href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UCSD_Pascal">UCSD=20
Pascal</A> <A title=3D"P-code machine" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/P-code_machine#UCSD_p-Machine">P-co=
de</A>=20
by two decades.</P>
<P>IBM later provided a FORTRAN IV compiler for the 1400 series of =
computers,=20
described in <A class=3D"external text" =
href=3D"http://www.bitsavers.org/pdf/ibm/140x/C24-3322-2_Fortran_IV_Langu=
age_Specifications_IBM_1401_1440_1460_Apr66.pdf"=20
rel=3D"nofollow">IBM document C24-3322</A>.</P>
<H3><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" id=3D"FORTRAN_IV">FORTRAN IV</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A title=3D"Edit section: =
FORTRAN IV" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran&amp;action=3D=
edit&amp;section=3D9">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H3>
<P>Starting in 1961, as a result of customer demands, IBM began =
development of a=20
<I>FORTRAN IV</I> that removed the machine-dependent features of FORTRAN =
II=20
(such as <CODE>READ INPUT TAPE</CODE>), while adding new features such =
as a <A=20
title=3D"Boolean data type"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boolean_data_type"><CODE>LOGICAL</C=
ODE> data=20
type</A>, logical <A title=3D"Boolean expression" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boolean_expression">Boolean=20
expressions</A> and the <I>logical IF statement</I> as an alternative to =
the=20
<I>arithmetic IF statement.</I> FORTRAN IV was eventually released in =
1962,=20
first for the <A title=3D"IBM 7030 Stretch" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IBM_7030_Stretch">IBM=20
7030</A> ("Stretch") computer, followed by versions for the <A =
title=3D"IBM 7090"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IBM_7090">IBM 7090</A>, <A =
title=3D"IBM 7090"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IBM_7090">IBM 7094</A>, and later =
for the <A=20
title=3D"IBM 1401" href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IBM_1401">IBM =
1401</A> in=20
1966.</P>
<P>By 1965, FORTRAN IV was supposed to be compliant with the =
<I>standard</I>=20
being developed by the <A title=3D"American National Standards =
Institute" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_National_Standards_Institu=
te">American=20
Standards Association</A> X3.4.3 FORTRAN Working Group.<SUP =
class=3D"reference"=20
id=3D"cite_ref-McCracken_12-0"><A =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran#cite_note-McCracken-12"><SP=
AN>[</SPAN>12<SPAN>]</SPAN></A></SUP></P>
<P>At about this time FORTRAN IV had started to become an important =
educational=20
tool and implementations such as the University of Waterloo's WATFOR and =
<A=20
title=3D"WATFIV" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/WATFIV">WATFIV</A> were=20
created to simplify the complex compile and link processes of earlier=20
compilers.</P>
<H3><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" id=3D"FORTRAN_66">FORTRAN 66</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A title=3D"Edit section: =
FORTRAN 66" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran&amp;action=3D=
edit&amp;section=3D10">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H3>
<P>Perhaps the most significant development in the early history of =
FORTRAN was=20
the decision by the <I>American Standards Association</I> (now <A =
title=3D"American National Standards Institute"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_National_Standards_Institu=
te">American=20
National Standards Institute</A> (ANSI)) to form a committee sponsored =
by BEMA,=20
the Business Equipment Manufacturers Association, to develop an =
<I>American=20
Standard Fortran</I>. The resulting two standards, approved in March =
1966,=20
defined two languages, <I>FORTRAN</I> (based on FORTRAN IV, which had =
served as=20
a de facto standard), and <I>Basic FORTRAN</I> (based on FORTRAN II, but =

stripped of its machine-dependent features). The FORTRAN defined by the =
first=20
standard, officially denoted X3.9-1966, became known as <I>FORTRAN =
66</I>=20
(although many continued to term it FORTRAN IV, the language on which =
the=20
standard was largely based). FORTRAN 66 effectively became the first=20
industry-standard version of FORTRAN. FORTRAN 66 included:</P>
<UL>
  <LI>Main program, <CODE>SUBROUTINE</CODE>, <CODE>FUNCTION</CODE>, and=20
  <CODE>BLOCK DATA</CODE> program units</LI>
  <LI><CODE>INTEGER</CODE>, <CODE>REAL</CODE>, <CODE>DOUBLE =
PRECISION</CODE>,=20
  <CODE>COMPLEX</CODE>, and <CODE>LOGICAL</CODE> <A title=3D"Data type" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Data_type">data=20
  types</A></LI>
  <LI><CODE>COMMON</CODE>, <CODE>DIMENSION</CODE>, and =
<CODE>EQUIVALENCE</CODE>=20
  statements</LI>
  <LI><CODE>DATA</CODE> statement for specifying initial values</LI>
  <LI><A title=3D"Intrinsic function" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Intrinsic_function">Intrinsic</A>=20
  and <CODE>EXTERNAL</CODE> (e.g., library) functions</LI>
  <LI>Assignment statement</LI>
  <LI><CODE>GOTO</CODE>, computed <CODE>GOTO</CODE>, assigned =
<CODE>GOTO</CODE>,=20
  and <CODE>ASSIGN</CODE> statements</LI>
  <LI>Logical <CODE>IF</CODE> and arithmetic (three-way) <CODE>IF</CODE> =

  statements</LI>
  <LI><CODE>DO</CODE> loops</LI>
  <LI><CODE>READ</CODE>, <CODE>WRITE</CODE>, <CODE>BACKSPACE</CODE>,=20
  <CODE>REWIND</CODE>, and <CODE>ENDFILE</CODE> statements for =
sequential=20
  I/O</LI>
  <LI><CODE>FORMAT</CODE> statement and assigned format</LI>
  <LI><CODE>CALL</CODE>, <CODE>RETURN</CODE>, <CODE>PAUSE</CODE>, and=20
  <CODE>STOP</CODE> statements</LI>
  <LI><A title=3D"Hollerith constant" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hollerith_constant">Hollerith=20
  constants</A> in <CODE>DATA</CODE> and <CODE>FORMAT</CODE> statements, =
and as=20
  arguments to procedures</LI>
  <LI>Identifiers of up to six characters in length</LI>
  <LI>Comment lines</LI></UL>
