compiler</DIV></DIV></DIV>
<P>After the release of the FORTRAN 66 standard, compiler vendors =
introduced=20
several extensions to <I>Standard Fortran</I>, prompting ANSI committee =
X3J3 in=20
1969 to begin work on revising the 1966 standard, under sponsorship of =
<A title=3D"CBEMA"=20
class=3D"mw-redirect" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CBEMA">CBEMA</A>, the=20
Computer Business Equipment Manufacturers Association (formerly BEMA). =
Final=20
drafts of this revised standard circulated in 1977, leading to formal =
approval=20
of the new FORTRAN standard in April 1978. The new standard, called =
<I>FORTRAN=20
77</I> and officially denoted X3.9-1978, added a number of significant =
features=20
to address many of the shortcomings of FORTRAN 66:</P>
<UL>
  <LI>Block <CODE>IF</CODE> and <CODE>END IF</CODE> statements, with =
optional=20
  <CODE>ELSE</CODE> and <CODE>ELSE IF</CODE> clauses, to provide =
improved=20
  language support for <A title=3D"Structured programming" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Structured_programming">structured =

  programming</A></LI>
  <LI>DO loop extensions, including parameter expressions, negative =
increments,=20
  and zero trip counts</LI>
  <LI><CODE>OPEN</CODE>, <CODE>CLOSE</CODE>, and <CODE>INQUIRE</CODE> =
statements=20
  for improved I/O capability</LI>
  <LI>Direct-access file I/O</LI>
  <LI><CODE>IMPLICIT</CODE> statement, to override implicit conventions =
that=20
  undeclared variables are INTEGER if their name begins with I, J, K, L, =
M, or N=20
  (and REAL otherwise)</LI>
  <LI><CODE>CHARACTER</CODE> data type, with vastly expanded facilities =
for=20
  character input and output and processing of character-based data</LI>
  <LI><CODE>PARAMETER</CODE> statement for specifying constants</LI>
  <LI><CODE>SAVE</CODE> statement for persistent local variables</LI>
  <LI>Generic names for intrinsic functions</LI>
  <LI>A set of intrinsics (<CODE>LGE, LGT, LLE, LLT</CODE>) for =
<U>lexical</U>=20
  comparison of strings, based upon the <A title=3D"ASCII" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ASCII">ASCII</A>=20
  <A title=3D"Collating sequence" class=3D"mw-redirect" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Collating_sequence">collating=20
  sequence</A>. (These ASCII functions were demanded by the <A =
title=3D"United States Department of Defense"=20
  =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Department_of_Defense=
">U.S.=20
  Department of Defense</A>, in their conditional approval vote.<SUP =
class=3D"noprint Inline-Template Template-Fact"=20
  style=3D"white-space: nowrap;">[<I><A title=3D"Wikipedia:Citation =
needed" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed"><SPAN=20
  title=3D"This claim needs references to reliable sources. (October =
2011)">citation=20
  needed</SPAN></A></I>]</SUP>)</LI></UL>
<P>In this revision of the standard, a number of features were removed =
or=20
altered in a manner that might invalidate formerly standard-conforming =
programs.=20
<I>(Removal was the only allowable alternative to X3J3 at that time, =
since the=20
concept of "<A title=3D"Deprecation" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deprecation">deprecation</A>"=20
was not yet available for ANSI standards.)</I> While most of the 24 =
items in the=20
conflict list (see Appendix A2 of X3.9-1978) addressed loopholes or =
pathological=20
cases permitted by the prior standard but rarely used, a small number of =

specific capabilities were deliberately removed, such as:</P>
<UL>
  <LI><A title=3D"Hollerith constant" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hollerith_constant">Hollerith=20
  constants</A> and <A title=3D"Herman Hollerith" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Herman_Hollerith">Hollerith</A>=20
  data, such as:</LI></UL>
<DL>
  <DD>
  <DL>
    <DD><TT>GREET =3D 12HHELLO THERE!</TT></DD></DL></DD></DL>
<UL>
  <LI>Reading into an H edit (Hollerith field) descriptor in a FORMAT=20
  specification</LI>
  <LI>Overindexing of array bounds by subscripts</LI></UL>
<DL>
  <DD>
  <DL>
    <DD><CODE>DIMENSION A(10,5)</CODE></DD>
    <DD><CODE>Y=3D A(11,1)</CODE></DD></DL></DD></DL>
<UL>
  <LI>Transfer of control out of and back into the range of a DO loop =
(also=20
  known as "Extended Range")</LI></UL>
<H4><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" =
id=3D"Variants:_Minnesota_FORTRAN">Variants:=20
Minnesota FORTRAN</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN =
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A=20
title=3D"Edit section: Variants: Minnesota FORTRAN" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran&amp;action=3D=
edit&amp;section=3D12">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H4>
<P><A title=3D"Control Data Corporation" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Control_Data_Corporation">Control=20
Data Corporation</A> computers had another version of FORTRAN 77, called =

Minnesota FORTRAN (MNF), designed especially for student use, with =
variations in=20
output constructs, special uses of COMMONs and DATA statements, =
optimizations=20
code levels for compiling, and detailed error listings, extensive =
warning=20
messages, and debugs.<SUP class=3D"reference" id=3D"cite_ref-13"><A =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran#cite_note-13"><SPAN>[</SPAN=
>13<SPAN>]</SPAN></A></SUP></P>
<H3><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline"=20
id=3D"Transition_to_ANSI_Standard_Fortran">Transition to ANSI Standard=20
Fortran</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A title=3D"Edit section: =
Transition to ANSI Standard Fortran"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran&amp;action=3D=
edit&amp;section=3D13">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H3>
<P>The development of a revised standard to succeed FORTRAN 77 would be=20
repeatedly delayed as the standardization process struggled to keep up =
with=20
rapid changes in computing and programming practice. In the meantime, as =
the=20
"Standard FORTRAN" for nearly fifteen years, FORTRAN 77 would become the =

historically most important dialect.</P>
<P>An important practical extension to FORTRAN 77 was the release of=20
MIL-STD-1753 in 1978.<SUP class=3D"reference" id=3D"cite_ref-14"><A =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran#cite_note-14"><SPAN>[</SPAN=
>14<SPAN>]</SPAN></A></SUP>=20
This specification, developed by the <A title=3D"United States =
Department of Defense"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Department_of_Defense=
">U.S.=20
Department of Defense</A>, standardized a number of features implemented =
by most=20
FORTRAN 77 compilers but not included in the ANSI FORTRAN 77 standard. =
These=20
features would eventually be incorporated into the Fortran 90 =
standard.</P>
<UL>
  <LI><CODE>DO WHILE</CODE> and <CODE>END DO</CODE> statements</LI>
  <LI><CODE>INCLUDE</CODE> statement</LI>
  <LI><CODE>IMPLICIT NONE</CODE> variant of the <CODE>IMPLICIT</CODE>=20
  statement</LI>
  <LI><A title=3D"Bit manipulation" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bit_manipulation">Bit=20
  manipulation</A> intrinsic functions, based on similar functions =
included in=20
  <A title=3D"Industrial Real-Time Fortran" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Industrial_Real-Time_Fortran">Indus=
trial=20
  Real-Time Fortran (ANSI/ISA S61.1 (1976))</A></LI></UL>
<P>The <A title=3D"Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Institute_of_Electrical_and_Electro=
nics_Engineers">IEEE</A>=20
1003.9 <A title=3D"POSIX" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/POSIX">POSIX</A>=20
Standard, released in 1991, provided a simple means for FORTRAN 77 =
programmers=20
to issue POSIX system calls.<SUP class=3D"reference" =
id=3D"cite_ref-15"><A =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran#cite_note-15"><SPAN>[</SPAN=
>15<SPAN>]</SPAN></A></SUP>=20
Over 100 calls were defined in the document =E2=80=94 allowing access to =

POSIX-compatible process control, signal handling, file system control, =
device=20
control, procedure pointing, and stream I/O in a portable manner.</P>
<H3><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" id=3D"Fortran_90">Fortran 90</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A title=3D"Edit section: =
Fortran 90" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran&amp;action=3D=
edit&amp;section=3D14">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H3>
<P>The much delayed successor to FORTRAN 77, informally known as =
<I>Fortran=20
90</I> (and prior to that, <I>Fortran 8X</I>), was finally released as =
ISO/IEC=20
standard 1539:1991 in 1991 and an ANSI Standard in 1992. In addition to =
changing=20
the official spelling from FORTRAN to Fortran, this major revision added =
many=20
new features to reflect the significant changes in programming practice =
that had=20
evolved since the 1978 standard:</P>
<UL>
  <LI><A title=3D"Free-form language" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Free-form_language">Free-form=20
  source input</A>, also with lowercase Fortran keywords</LI>
  <LI>Identifiers up to 31 characters in length</LI>
  <LI>Inline comments</LI>
  <LI>Ability to operate on arrays (or array sections) as a whole, thus =
greatly=20
  simplifying math and engineering computations.=20
  <UL>
    <LI>whole, partial and masked array assignment statements and array=20
    expressions, such as &nbsp; =
<CODE>X(1:N)=3DR(1:N)*COS(A(1:N))</CODE></LI>
    <LI><CODE>WHERE</CODE> statement for selective array assignment</LI>
    <LI>array-valued constants and expressions,</LI>
    <LI>user-defined array-valued functions and array =
constructors.</LI></UL></LI>
  <LI><A title=3D"Recursion (computer science)" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Recursion_(computer_science)"><CODE=
>RECURSIVE</CODE></A>=20
  procedures</LI>
  <LI><A title=3D"Modular programming" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Modular_programming">Modules</A>,=20
  to group related <A title=3D"Subroutine" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Subroutine">procedures</A>=20
  and data together, and make them available to other program units, =
including=20
  the capability to limit the accessibility to only specific parts of =
the=20
  module.</LI>
  <LI>A vastly improved argument-passing mechanism, allowing <A =
title=3D"Type signature"=20
  href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Type_signature">interfaces</A> =
to be=20
  checked at compile time</LI>
  <LI>User-written interfaces for generic procedures</LI>
  <LI><A title=3D"Operator overloading" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operator_overloading">Operator=20
  overloading</A></LI>
  <LI>Derived (structured) data types</LI>
  <LI>New data type declaration syntax, to specify the data type and =
other=20
  attributes of variables</LI>
  <LI><A title=3D"Dynamic memory allocation" class=3D"mw-redirect" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dynamic_memory_allocation">Dynamic =

  memory allocation</A> by means of the <CODE>ALLOCATABLE</CODE> =
attribute and=20
  the <CODE>ALLOCATE</CODE> and <CODE>DEALLOCATE</CODE> statements</LI>
  <LI><A title=3D"Pointer (computer programming)" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pointer_(computer_programming)"><CO=
DE>POINTER</CODE></A>=20
  attribute, pointer assignment, and <CODE>NULLIFY</CODE> statement to=20
  facilitate the creation and manipulation of dynamic <A title=3D"Data =
structure"=20
  href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Data_structure">data =
structures</A></LI>
  <LI>Structured looping constructs, with an <CODE>END DO</CODE> =
statement for=20
  loop termination, and <CODE>EXIT</CODE> and <CODE>CYCLE</CODE> =
statements for=20
  terminating normal <CODE>DO</CODE> loop iterations in an orderly =
way</LI>
  <LI><CODE>SELECT</CODE> . . . <CODE>CASE</CODE> construct for =
multi-way=20
  selection</LI>
  <LI>Portable specification of numerical precision under the user's=20
control</LI>
  <LI>New and enhanced intrinsic procedures.</LI></UL>
<H4><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" =
id=3D"Obsolescence_and_deletions">Obsolescence and=20
deletions</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN =
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A=20
title=3D"Edit section: Obsolescence and deletions" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran&amp;action=3D=
edit&amp;section=3D15">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H4>
<P>Unlike the prior revision, Fortran 90 removed no features. =
<I>(Appendix B.1=20
says, "The list of deleted features in this standard is empty.")</I> Any =

standard-conforming FORTRAN 77 program is also standard-conforming under =
Fortran=20
90, and either standard should be usable to define its behavior.</P>
<P>A small set of features were identified as "obsolescent" and expected =
to be=20
removed in a future standard.</P>
<TABLE class=3D"wikitable">
  <TBODY>
  <TR>
    <TH>Obsolescent feature</TH>
    <TH>Example</TH>
    <TH>Status/fate in Fortran 95</TH></TR>
  <TR>
    <TD>Arithmetic IF-statement</TD>
    <TD><CODE>&nbsp; &nbsp; IF (X) 10, 20, 30</CODE></TD>
    <TD></TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TD>Non-integer DO parameters or control variables</TD>
    <TD><CODE>&nbsp; &nbsp; DO 9 X=3D 1.7, 1.6, -0.1</CODE></TD>
    <TD>Deleted</TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TD>Shared DO-loop termination or<BR>termination with a =
statement<BR>other=20
      than END DO or CONTINUE &nbsp;</TD>
    <TD>
<PRE>    DO 9 J=3D 1, 10=0A=
        DO 9 K=3D 1, 10=0A=
9       L=3D  J + K=0A=
</PRE></TD>
    <TD></TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TD>Branching to END IF<BR>
      <P>from outside a block</P></TD>
    <TD>
<PRE>66  GO TO 77 ; . . .=0A=
    IF (E) THEN ;     . . .=0A=
77   END IF=0A=
</PRE></TD>
    <TD>Deleted</TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TD>Alternate return</TD>
    <TD><CODE>&nbsp; &nbsp; CALL SUBR( X, Y *100, *200 )</CODE></TD>
    <TD></TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TD>PAUSE statement</TD>
    <TD><CODE>&nbsp; &nbsp; PAUSE 600</CODE></TD>
    <TD>Deleted</TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TD>ASSIGN statement<BR>&nbsp; and assigned GO TO statement</TD>
    <TD>
<PRE>100    . . .=0A=
    ASSIGN 100 TO H=0A=
    . . .=0A=
    GO TO H . . .=0A=
</PRE></TD>
    <TD>Deleted</TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TD>Assigned FORMAT specifiers</TD>
    <TD><CODE>&nbsp; &nbsp; ASSIGN 606 TO F ... WRITE ( 6, F =
)...</CODE></TD>
    <TD>Deleted</TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TD>H edit descriptors</TD>
    <TD><CODE>606&nbsp;FORMAT ( 9H1GOODBYE. )</CODE></TD>
    <TD>Deleted</TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TD>Computed GO TO statement</TD>
    <TD><CODE>&nbsp; &nbsp; GO TO (10, 20, 30, 40), index</CODE></TD>
    <TD>(obsolete)</TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TD>Statement functions</TD>
    <TD><CODE>&nbsp; &nbsp; FOIL( X, Y )=3D X**2 + 2*X*Y + =
Y**2</CODE></TD>
    <TD>(obsolete)</TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TD>DATA statements<BR>&nbsp; among executable statements</TD>
    <TD>
<PRE>    X=3D 27.3=0A=
    DATA  A, B, C  / 5.0, 12.0. 13.0 /=0A=
    . . .=0A=
</PRE></TD>
    <TD>(obsolete)</TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TD>CHARACTER* form of CHARACTER declaration</TD>
    <TD><CODE>&nbsp; &nbsp; CHARACTER*8 STRING &nbsp;&nbsp;! Use=20
      CHARACTER(8)</CODE></TD>
    <TD>(obsolete)</TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TD>Assumed character length functions</TD>
    <TD><CODE>&nbsp; &nbsp; CHARACTER*(*) STRING</CODE></TD>
    <TD>(obsolete)<SUP class=3D"reference" =
id=3D"cite_ref-assume_16-0"><A =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran#cite_note-assume-16"><SPAN>=
[</SPAN>16<SPAN>]</SPAN></A></SUP></TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TD>Fixed form source code</TD>
    <TD>Column 1 contains *,&nbsp;! or C for comments.<BR>Column 6 for=20
      continuation.</TD>
    <TD></TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE>
<H4><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" id=3D".22Hello_world.22_example">"Hello =
world"=20
example</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A title=3D'Edit section: =
"Hello world" example'=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran&amp;action=3D=
edit&amp;section=3D16">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H4>
<DIV class=3D"mw-highlight mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"ltr">
<PRE><SPAN class=3D"k">program </SPAN><SPAN class=3D"n">helloworld</SPAN>=0A=
     <SPAN class=3D"k">print</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"o">*</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"s2">"Hello world!"</SPAN>=0A=
<SPAN class=3D"k">end program </SPAN><SPAN class=3D"n">helloworld</SPAN>=0A=
