<H3><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" id=3D"Fortran_95">Fortran 95</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A title=3D"Edit section: =
Fortran 95" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran&amp;action=3D=
edit&amp;section=3D17">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H3>
<DIV class=3D"hatnote relarticle mainarticle">Main article: <A =
title=3D"Fortran 95 language features"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran_95_language_features">Fortr=
an 95=20
language features</A></DIV>
<P><I>Fortran 95,</I> published officially as ISO/IEC 1539-1:1997, was a =
minor=20
revision, mostly to resolve some outstanding issues from the Fortran 90=20
standard. Nevertheless, Fortran 95 also added a number of extensions, =
notably=20
from the <A title=3D"High Performance Fortran" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/High_Performance_Fortran">High=20
Performance Fortran</A> specification:</P>
<UL>
  <LI><CODE>FORALL</CODE> and nested <CODE>WHERE</CODE> constructs to =
aid=20
  vectorization</LI>
  <LI>User-defined <CODE>PURE</CODE> and <CODE>ELEMENTAL</CODE> =
procedures</LI>
  <LI>Default initialization of derived type components, including =
pointer=20
  initialization</LI>
  <LI>Expanded the ability to use initialization expressions for data=20
  objects</LI>
  <LI>Initialization of pointers to <CODE>NULL()</CODE></LI>
  <LI>Clearly defined that <CODE>ALLOCATABLE</CODE> arrays are =
automatically=20
  deallocated when they go out of scope.</LI></UL>
<P>A number of intrinsic functions were extended (for example a =
<CODE>dim</CODE>=20
argument was added to the <CODE>maxloc</CODE> intrinsic).</P>
<P>Several features noted in Fortran 90 to be "obsolescent" were removed =
from=20
Fortran 95:</P>
<UL>
  <LI><CODE>DO</CODE> statements using <CODE>REAL</CODE> and =
<CODE>DOUBLE=20
  PRECISION</CODE> variables</LI>
  <LI>Branching to an <CODE>END IF</CODE> statement from outside its =
block</LI>
  <LI><CODE>PAUSE</CODE> statement</LI>
  <LI><CODE>ASSIGN</CODE> and assigned <CODE>GOTO</CODE> statement, and =
assigned=20
  format specifiers</LI>
  <LI><CODE>H</CODE> edit descriptor.</LI></UL>
<P>An important supplement to Fortran 95 was the <A =
title=3D"International Organization for Standardization"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_Organization_for_Stan=
dardization">ISO=20
technical report</A> <I>TR-15581: Enhanced Data Type Facilities</I>, =
informally=20
known as the <I>Allocatable TR.</I> This specification defined enhanced =
use of=20
<CODE>ALLOCATABLE</CODE> arrays, prior to the availability of fully =
Fortran=20
2003-compliant Fortran compilers. Such uses include =
<CODE>ALLOCATABLE</CODE>=20
arrays as derived type components, in procedure dummy argument lists, =
and as=20
function return values. (<CODE>ALLOCATABLE</CODE> arrays are preferable =
to=20
<CODE>POINTER</CODE>-based arrays because <CODE>ALLOCATABLE</CODE> =
arrays are=20
guaranteed by Fortran 95 to be deallocated automatically when they go =
out of=20
scope, eliminating the possibility of <A title=3D"Memory leak" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Memory_leak">memory=20
leakage</A>. In addition, elements of allocatable arrays are contiguous, =
and <A=20
title=3D"Aliasing (computing)" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aliasing_(computing)">aliasing</A> =

is not an issue for optimization of array references, allowing compilers =
to=20
generate faster code than in the case of pointers.<SUP =
class=3D"reference" id=3D"cite_ref-17"><A=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran#cite_note-17"><SPAN>[</SPAN=
>17<SPAN>]</SPAN></A></SUP>)</P>
<P>Another important supplement to Fortran 95 was the <A =
title=3D"International Organization for Standardization"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_Organization_for_Stan=
dardization">ISO</A>=20
technical report <I>TR-15580: Floating-point exception handling</I>, =
informally=20
known as the <I>IEEE TR.</I> This specification defined support for <A =
title=3D"IEEE 754-2008"=20
class=3D"mw-redirect" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IEEE_754-2008">IEEE=20
floating-point arithmetic</A> and <A title=3D"Floating point" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Floating_point">floating=20
point</A> <A title=3D"Exception handling" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exception_handling">exception=20
handling</A>.</P>
<H4><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" =
id=3D"Conditional_compilation_and_varying_length_strings">Conditional=20
compilation and varying length strings</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A title=3D"Edit section: =
Conditional compilation and varying length strings"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran&amp;action=3D=
edit&amp;section=3D18">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H4>
<P>In addition to the mandatory "Base language" (defined in ISO/IEC=20
1539-1&nbsp;: 1997), the Fortran 95 language also includes two optional=20
modules:</P>
<UL>
  <LI>Varying character strings (ISO/IEC 1539-2&nbsp;: 2000)</LI>
  <LI>Conditional compilation (ISO/IEC 1539-3&nbsp;: 1998)</LI></UL>
<P>which, together, compose the multi-part International Standard =
(ISO/IEC=20
1539).</P>
<P>According to the standards developers, "the optional parts describe=20
self-contained features which have been requested by a substantial body =
of users=20
and/or implementors, but which are not deemed to be of sufficient =
generality for=20
them to be required in all standard-conforming Fortran compilers." =
Nevertheless,=20
if a standard-conforming Fortran does provide such options, then they =
"must be=20
provided in accordance with the description of those facilities in the=20
appropriate Part of the Standard."</P>
<H3><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" id=3D"Fortran_2003">Fortran =
2003</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A title=3D"Edit section: =
Fortran 2003"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran&amp;action=3D=
edit&amp;section=3D19">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H3>
<P><I>Fortran 2003,</I> officially published as ISO/IEC 1539-1:2004, is =
a major=20
revision introducing many new features.<SUP class=3D"reference"=20
id=3D"cite_ref-18"><A=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran#cite_note-18"><SPAN>[</SPAN=
>18<SPAN>]</SPAN></A></SUP>=20
A comprehensive summary of the new features of Fortran 2003 is available =
at the=20
Fortran Working Group (ISO/IEC JTC1/SC22/WG5) official Web site.<SUP =
class=3D"reference"=20
id=3D"cite_ref-19"><A=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran#cite_note-19"><SPAN>[</SPAN=
>19<SPAN>]</SPAN></A></SUP></P>
<P>From that article, the major enhancements for this revision =
include:</P>
<UL>
  <LI>Derived type enhancements: parameterized derived types, improved =
control=20
  of accessibility, improved structure constructors, and finalizers</LI>
  <LI><A title=3D"Object-oriented programming" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Object-oriented_programming">Object=
-oriented=20
  programming</A> support: <A title=3D"Inheritance (computer science)" =
class=3D"mw-redirect"=20
  =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inheritance_(computer_science)">typ=
e=20
  extension and inheritance</A>, <A title=3D"Type polymorphism" =
class=3D"mw-redirect"=20
  =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Type_polymorphism">polymorphism</A>=
,=20
  dynamic type allocation, and type-bound procedures, providing complete =
support=20
  for <A title=3D"Abstract data type" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abstract_data_type">abstract=20
  data types</A></LI>
  <LI>Data manipulation enhancements: allocatable components =
(incorporating TR=20
  15581), deferred type parameters, <A title=3D"Volatile variable" =
class=3D"mw-redirect"=20
  =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Volatile_variable"><CODE>VOLATILE</=
CODE>=20
  attribute</A>, explicit type specification in array constructors and =
allocate=20
  statements, pointer enhancements, extended initialization expressions, =
and=20
  enhanced intrinsic procedures</LI>
  <LI>Input/output enhancements: <A title=3D"Asynchronous I/O" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Asynchronous_I/O">asynchronous</A> =

  transfer, stream access, user specified transfer operations for =
derived types,=20
  user specified control of rounding during format conversions, named =
constants=20
  for preconnected units, the <CODE>FLUSH</CODE> statement, =
regularization of=20
  keywords, and access to error messages</LI>
  <LI><A title=3D"Function pointer" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Function_pointer">Procedure=20
  pointers</A></LI>
  <LI>Support for <A title=3D"IEEE 754-2008" class=3D"mw-redirect" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IEEE_754-2008">IEEE=20
  floating-point arithmetic</A> and <A title=3D"Floating point" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Floating_point">floating=20
  point</A> <A title=3D"Exception handling" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exception_handling">exception=20
  handling</A> (incorporating TR 15580)</LI>
  <LI>Interoperability with the <A title=3D"C (programming language)" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C_(programming_language)">C=20
  programming language</A></LI>
  <LI>Support for international usage: access to <A title=3D"Universal =
Character Set"=20
  class=3D"mw-redirect" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Universal_Character_Set">ISO=20
  10646</A> 4-byte characters and choice of decimal or comma in numeric=20
  formatted input/output</LI>
  <LI>Enhanced integration with the host operating system: access to <A =
title=3D"Command-line interface"=20
  href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Command-line_interface">command =
line</A>=20
  arguments, <A title=3D"Environment variable" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Environment_variable">environment=20
  variables</A>, and processor error messages</LI></UL>
<P>An important supplement to Fortran 2003 was the <A =
title=3D"International Organization for Standardization"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_Organization_for_Stan=
dardization">ISO=20
technical report</A> <I>TR-19767: Enhanced module facilities in =
Fortran.</I>=20
This report provided <I>submodules,</I> which make Fortran modules more =
similar=20
to <A title=3D"Modula-2"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Modula-2">Modula-2</A> modules. =
They are=20
similar to <A title=3D"Ada (programming language)" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ada_(programming_language)">Ada</A>=
=20
private child subunits. This allows the specification and implementation =
of a=20
module to be expressed in separate program units, which improves =
packaging of=20
large libraries, allows preservation of trade secrets while publishing=20
definitive interfaces, and prevents compilation cascades.</P>
<H3><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" id=3D"Fortran_2008">Fortran =
2008</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A title=3D"Edit section: =
Fortran 2008"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran&amp;action=3D=
edit&amp;section=3D20">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H3>
<P>The most recent standard, ISO/IEC 1539-1:2010, informally known as =
Fortran=20
2008, was approved in September 2010.<SUP class=3D"reference" =
id=3D"cite_ref-20"><A=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran#cite_note-20"><SPAN>[</SPAN=
>20<SPAN>]</SPAN></A></SUP><SUP=20
class=3D"reference" id=3D"cite_ref-21"><A =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran#cite_note-21"><SPAN>[</SPAN=
>21<SPAN>]</SPAN></A></SUP>=20
As with Fortran 95, this is a minor upgrade, incorporating =
clarifications and=20
corrections to Fortran 2003, as well as introducing a select few new=20
capabilities. The new capabilities include:</P>
<UL>
  <LI>Submodules =E2=80=93 Additional structuring facilities for =
modules; supersedes=20
  ISO/IEC TR 19767:2005</LI>
  <LI><A title=3D"Coarray Fortran" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coarray_Fortran">Coarray=20
  Fortran</A> =E2=80=93 a parallel execution model</LI>
  <LI>The <A title=3D"Scalable parallelism" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scalable_parallelism">DO=20
  CONCURRENT</A> construct =E2=80=93 for loop iterations with no =
interdependencies</LI>
  <LI>The CONTIGUOUS attribute =E2=80=93 to specify storage layout =
restrictions</LI>
  <LI>The <A title=3D"Dynamic scoping" class=3D"mw-redirect" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dynamic_scoping">BLOCK=20
  construct</A> =E2=80=93 can contain declarations of objects with =
construct scope</LI>
  <LI><A title=3D"Recursive data type" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Recursive_data_type">Recursive=20
  allocatable components</A> =E2=80=93 as an alternative to recursive =
pointers in=20
  derived types</LI></UL>
<P>The Final Draft international Standard (FDIS) is available as =
document=20
N1830.<SUP class=3D"reference" id=3D"cite_ref-22"><A =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran#cite_note-22"><SPAN>[</SPAN=
>22<SPAN>]</SPAN></A></SUP></P>
<P>An important supplement to Fortran 2008 is the <A =
title=3D"International Organization for Standardization"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_Organization_for_Stan=
dardization">ISO</A>=20
Technical Specification (TS) 29113 on <I>Further Interoperability of =
Fortran=20
with C</I>,<SUP class=3D"reference" id=3D"cite_ref-23"><A =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran#cite_note-23"><SPAN>[</SPAN=
>23<SPAN>]</SPAN></A></SUP><SUP=20
class=3D"reference" id=3D"cite_ref-24"><A =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran#cite_note-24"><SPAN>[</SPAN=
>24<SPAN>]</SPAN></A></SUP>=20
which has been submitted to ISO in May 2012 for approval. The =
specification adds=20
support for accessing the array descriptor from C and allows ignoring =
the type=20
and rank of arguments.</P>
<H3><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" id=3D"Fortran_2015">Fortran =
2015</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A title=3D"Edit section: =
Fortran 2015"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran&amp;action=3D=
edit&amp;section=3D21">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H3>
<P>The next revision of the language (Fortran 2015) is intended to be a =
minor=20
revision and is planned for release in mid-2018.<SUP class=3D"reference" =
id=3D"cite_ref-Fortran2015Closes_25-0"><A=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran#cite_note-Fortran2015Closes=
-25"><SPAN>[</SPAN>25<SPAN>]</SPAN></A></SUP>=20
It is currently planned to include further interoperability between =
Fortran and=20
C, additional parallel features, and "the removal of simple deficiencies =
in and=20
discrepancies between existing facilities."<SUP class=3D"reference" =
id=3D"cite_ref-Fortran2015_26-0"><A=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran#cite_note-Fortran2015-26"><=
SPAN>[</SPAN>26<SPAN>]</SPAN></A></SUP><SUP=20
class=3D"reference" id=3D"cite_ref-27"><A =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran#cite_note-27"><SPAN>[</SPAN=
>27<SPAN>]</SPAN></A></SUP></P>
<H2><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" =
id=3D"Fortran_and_supercomputers">Fortran and=20
supercomputers</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN =
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A=20
title=3D"Edit section: Fortran and supercomputers" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran&amp;action=3D=
edit&amp;section=3D22">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H2>
<P>Since Fortran has been in use for more than fifty years, there is a =
vast body=20
of Fortran software in daily use throughout the scientific and =
engineering=20
communities.<SUP class=3D"reference" id=3D"cite_ref-28"><A =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran#cite_note-28"><SPAN>[</SPAN=
>28<SPAN>]</SPAN></A></SUP>=20
<A title=3D"Jay Pasachoff" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jay_Pasachoff">Jay=20
Pasachoff</A> wrote in 1984 that "physics and astronomy students simply =
have to=20
learn Fortran. So much exists in Fortran that it seems unlikely that =
scientists=20
will change to Pascal, Modula-2, or whatever."<SUP class=3D"reference" =
id=3D"cite_ref-pasachoff198404_29-0"><A=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran#cite_note-pasachoff198404-2=
9"><SPAN>[</SPAN>29<SPAN>]</SPAN></A></SUP>=20
In 1993, Cecil E. Leith called Fortran the "mother tongue of scientific=20
computing" adding that its replacement by any other possible language =
"may=20
remain a forlorn hope."<SUP class=3D"reference" =
id=3D"cite_ref-Galperin_30-0"><A=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran#cite_note-Galperin-30"><SPA=
N>[</SPAN>30<SPAN>]</SPAN></A></SUP>=20
It is the primary language for some of the most intensive <A =
title=3D"Supercomputer"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Supercomputer">supercomputing</A> =
tasks,=20
such as <A title=3D"Astronomy"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Astronomy">astronomy</A>, <A =
title=3D"Numerical weather prediction"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Numerical_weather_prediction">weath=
er</A>=20
and <A title=3D"Climate model"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Climate_model">climate =
modeling</A>,=20
numerical linear algebra (<A title=3D"LAPACK" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/LAPACK">LAPACK</A>),=20
numerical libraries (<A title=3D"IMSL Numerical Libraries" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IMSL_Numerical_Libraries">IMSL</A> =

and <A title=3D"NAG Numerical Library" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NAG_Numerical_Library">NAG</A>),=20
<A title=3D"Structural engineering" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Structural_engineering">structural =

engineering</A>, <A title=3D"Hydrological modeling" =
class=3D"mw-redirect" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hydrological_modeling">hydrological=
=20
modeling</A>, <A title=3D"Optimization" class=3D"mw-redirect" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Optimization">optimization</A>,=20
satellite simulation and data analysis, <A title=3D"Computational fluid =
dynamics"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computational_fluid_dynamics">compu=
tational=20
fluid dynamics</A>, <A title=3D"Computational chemistry" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computational_chemistry">computatio=
nal=20
chemistry</A>, <A title=3D"Computational economics" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computational_economics">computatio=
nal=20
economics</A> and <A title=3D"Computational physics" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computational_physics">computationa=
l=20
physics</A>. A half century after its creation, many of the =
floating-point=20
benchmarks to gauge the performance of new computer =
processors=E2=80=94such as <A class=3D"external text"=20
href=3D"http://www.spec.org/cpu2006/CFP2006/" =
rel=3D"nofollow">CFP2006</A>, the=20
floating-point component of the <A title=3D"Standard Performance =
Evaluation Corporation"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Standard_Performance_Evaluation_Cor=
poration">SPEC</A>=20
<A class=3D"external text" href=3D"http://www.spec.org/cpu2006/"=20
rel=3D"nofollow">CPU2006</A> benchmarks=E2=80=94are still written in =
Fortran.</P>
<P>On the other hand, more modern code generally uses large program =
libraries=20
such as <A title=3D"PETSc" class=3D"mw-redirect" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PETSc">PETSc</A>=20
or <A title=3D"Trilinos"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trilinos">Trilinos</A> for linear =
algebra=20
capabilities, <A title=3D"METIS"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/METIS">METIS</A> for graph =
partitioning, <A=20
title=3D"Deal.II" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deal.II">deal.II</A> or <A=20
title=3D"FEniCS Project"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FEniCS_Project">FEniCS</A> for =
mesh and=20
finite element support, and other generic libraries. Since the late =
1990s,=20
almost all of the most widely used support libraries have been written =
in <A=20
title=3D"C (programming language)" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C_(programming_language)">C</A>=20
and, more often, <A title=3D"C++"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C%2B%2B">C++</A>. Consequently, a =
growing=20
fraction of scientific code is also written in these languages. For this =
reason,=20
facilities for interoperation with C were added to Fortran 2003, and =
enhanced by=20
ISO/IEC technical specification 29113, which will be incorporated into =
Fortran=20
2015. This shift is also evident in the selection of applications =
between the <A=20
title=3D"SPECfp" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SPECfp#Benchmarks_2">SPEC CPU=20
2000</A> and <A title=3D"SPECfp" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SPECfp#Benchmarks">SPEC=20
CPU 2006</A> floating point benchmarks.</P>
<H2><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" id=3D"Language_features">Language=20
features</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A title=3D"Edit section: =
Language features"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran&amp;action=3D=
edit&amp;section=3D23">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H2>
<DIV class=3D"hatnote relarticle mainarticle">Main article: <A =
title=3D"Fortran language features"=20
class=3D"mw-redirect" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran_language_features">Fortran =

language features</A></DIV>
<P>The precise characteristics and syntax of Fortran are discussed in a =
separate=20
article.</P>
<H2><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" =
id=3D"Portability">Portability</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A title=3D"Edit section: =
Portability"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran&amp;action=3D=
edit&amp;section=3D24">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H2>
<TABLE class=3D"metadata plainlinks ambox ambox-content =
ambox-Unreferenced" role=3D"presentation">
  <TBODY>
  <TR>
    <TD class=3D"mbox-image">
      <DIV style=3D"width: 52px;"><A class=3D"image" =
