2011)</I></SMALL></SPAN></TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE>
<P><A title=3D"Portability (computer science)" class=3D"mw-redirect" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portability_(computer_science)">Por=
tability</A>=20
was a problem in the early days because there was no agreed =
standard=E2=80=94not even=20
IBM's reference manual=E2=80=94and computer companies vied to =
differentiate their=20
offerings from others by providing incompatible features. Standards have =

improved portability. The 1966 standard provided a reference <A =
title=3D"Syntax (programming languages)"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Syntax_(programming_languages)">syn=
tax</A>=20
and semantics, but vendors continued to provide incompatible extensions. =

Although careful programmers were coming to realize that use of =
incompatible=20
extensions caused expensive portability problems, and were therefore =
using=20
programs such as <I>The PFORT Verifier,</I> it was not until after the =
1977=20
standard, when the National Bureau of Standards (now <A =
title=3D"National Institute of Standards and Technology"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Institute_of_Standards_and=
_Technology">NIST</A>)=20
published <I>FIPS PUB 69</I>, that processors purchased by the U.S. =
Government=20
were required to diagnose extensions of the standard. Rather than offer =
two=20
processors, essentially every compiler eventually had at least an option =
to=20
diagnose extensions.</P>
<P>Incompatible extensions were not the only portability problem. For =
numerical=20
calculations, it is important to take account of the characteristics of =
the=20
arithmetic. This was addressed by Fox et al. in the context of the 1966 =
standard=20
by the <I>PORT</I> library. The ideas therein became widely used, and =
were=20
eventually incorporated into the 1990 standard by way of intrinsic =
inquiry=20
functions. The widespread (now almost universal) adoption of the <A =
title=3D"IEEE 754-2008"=20
class=3D"mw-redirect" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IEEE_754-2008">IEEE=20
754</A> standard for binary floating-point arithmetic has essentially =
removed=20
this problem.</P>
<P>Access to the computing environment (e.g., the program's command =
line,=20
environment variables, textual explanation of error conditions) remained =
a=20
problem until it was addressed by the 2003 standard.</P>
<P>Large collections of library software that could be described as =
being=20
loosely related to engineering and scientific calculations, such as =
graphics=20
libraries, have been written in C, and therefore access to them =
presented a=20
portability problem. This has been addressed by incorporation of C=20
interoperability into the 2003 standard.</P>
<P>It is now possible (and relatively easy) to write an entirely =
portable=20
program in Fortran, even without recourse to a preprocessor.</P>
<H2><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" id=3D"Variants">Variants</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A title=3D"Edit section: =
Variants" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran&amp;action=3D=
edit&amp;section=3D25">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H2>
<H3><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" id=3D"Fortran_5">Fortran 5</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A title=3D"Edit section: =
Fortran 5" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran&amp;action=3D=
edit&amp;section=3D26">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H3>
<P>Fortran 5 was marketed by <A title=3D"Data General" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Data_General">Data=20
General</A> Corp in the late 1970s and early 1980s, for the <A =
title=3D"Data General Nova"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Data_General_Nova">Nova</A>, <A =
title=3D"Data General Eclipse"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Data_General_Eclipse">Eclipse</A>, =
and <A=20
title=3D"Data General Eclipse MV/8000" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Data_General_Eclipse_MV/8000">MV</A=
>=20
line of computers. It had an optimizing compiler that was quite good for =

minicomputers of its time. The language most closely resembles Fortran =
66. The=20
name is a <A title=3D"Pun" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pun">pun</A> on the=20
earlier Fortran IV.</P>
<H3><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" id=3D"Fortran_V">Fortran V</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A title=3D"Edit section: =
Fortran V" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran&amp;action=3D=
edit&amp;section=3D27">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H3>
<P>Fortran V was distributed by <A title=3D"Control Data Corporation" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Control_Data_Corporation">Control=20
Data Corporation</A> in 1968 for the <A title=3D"CDC 6600" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CDC_6600">CDC=20
6600</A> series. The language was based upon Fortran IV.<SUP =
class=3D"reference"=20
id=3D"cite_ref-FIV_31-0"><A =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran#cite_note-FIV-31"><SPAN>[</=
SPAN>31<SPAN>]</SPAN></A></SUP></P>
<P>Univac also offered a compiler for the 1100 series known as Fortran =
V. A=20
spinoff of Univac Fortran V was Athena Fortran.</P>
<H3><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" id=3D"Fortran_6">Fortran 6</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A title=3D"Edit section: =
Fortran 6" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran&amp;action=3D=
edit&amp;section=3D28">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H3>
<P><B>Fortran 6</B> or Visual Fortran 2001 was licensed to <A =
title=3D"Compaq"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Compaq">Compaq</A> by Microsoft. =
They have=20
licensed Compaq Visual Fortran and have provided the Visual Studio 5 =
environment=20
interface for <A title=3D"Visual Studio 97" class=3D"mw-redirect" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Visual_Studio_97">Compaq=20
v6</A> up to v6.1.<SUP class=3D"reference" id=3D"cite_ref-32"><A =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran#cite_note-32"><SPAN>[</SPAN=
>32<SPAN>]</SPAN></A></SUP></P>
<H3><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" id=3D"Specific_variants">Specific=20
variants</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A title=3D"Edit section: =
Specific variants"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran&amp;action=3D=
edit&amp;section=3D29">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H3>
<P>Vendors of high-performance scientific computers (<I>e.g.,</I> <A =
title=3D"Burroughs Corporation"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Burroughs_Corporation">Burroughs</A=
>, <A=20
title=3D"Control Data Corporation" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Control_Data_Corporation">Control=20
Data Corporation</A> (CDC), <A title=3D"Cray" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cray">Cray</A>,=20
<A title=3D"Honeywell"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Honeywell">Honeywell</A>, <A =
title=3D"IBM"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IBM">IBM</A>, <A title=3D"Texas =
Instruments"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Texas_Instruments">Texas =
Instruments</A>,=20
and <A title=3D"UNIVAC" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UNIVAC">UNIVAC</A>)=20
added extensions to Fortran to take advantage of special hardware =
features such=20
as <A title=3D"CPU cache"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CPU_cache">instruction cache</A>, =
CPU <A=20
title=3D"Pipeline (computing)" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pipeline_(computing)">pipelines</A>=
,=20
and vector arrays. For example, one of IBM's FORTRAN compilers (<I>H =
Extended=20
IUP</I>) had a level of optimization which reordered the <A =
title=3D"Machine code"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Machine_code">machine code</A> <A =
title=3D"Instruction (computer science)"=20
class=3D"mw-redirect" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Instruction_(computer_science)">ins=
tructions</A>=20
to keep multiple internal arithmetic units busy simultaneously. Another =
example=20
is <I>CFD</I>, a special variant of Fortran designed specifically for =
the <A=20
title=3D"ILLIAC IV" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ILLIAC_IV">ILLIAC IV</A>=20
supercomputer, running at <A title=3D"NASA" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NASA">NASA</A>'s=20
<A title=3D"NASA Ames Research Center" class=3D"mw-redirect" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NASA_Ames_Research_Center">Ames=20
Research Center</A>. IBM Research Labs also developed an extended =
FORTRAN-based=20
language called <I>VECTRAN</I> for processing vectors and matrices.</P>
<P><A title=3D"Object-Oriented Fortran" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Object-Oriented_Fortran">Object-Ori=
ented=20
Fortran</A> was an object-oriented extension of Fortran, in which data =
items can=20
be grouped into objects, which can be instantiated and executed in =
parallel. It=20
was available for Sun, Iris, <A title=3D"Intel iPSC" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Intel_iPSC">iPSC</A>,=20
and nCUBE, but is no longer supported.</P>
<P>Such machine-specific extensions have either disappeared over time or =
have=20
had elements incorporated into the main standards. The major remaining =
extension=20
is <A title=3D"OpenMP" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OpenMP">OpenMP</A>,=20
which is a cross-platform extension for shared memory programming. One =
new=20
extension, <A title=3D"Coarray Fortran" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coarray_Fortran">Coarray=20
Fortran</A>, is intended to support parallel programming.</P>
<H4><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" id=3D"FOR_TRANSIT_for_the_IBM_650">FOR =
TRANSIT for=20
the IBM 650</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN =
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A=20
title=3D"Edit section: FOR TRANSIT for the IBM 650" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran&amp;action=3D=
edit&amp;section=3D30">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H4>
<P><I>FOR TRANSIT</I> was the name of a reduced version of the IBM 704 =
FORTRAN=20
language, which was implemented for the IBM 650, using a translator =
program=20
developed at Carnegie in the late 1950s.<SUP class=3D"reference"=20
id=3D"cite_ref-33"><A=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran#cite_note-33"><SPAN>[</SPAN=
>33<SPAN>]</SPAN></A></SUP>=20
The following comment appears in the IBM Reference Manual (<I>FOR =
TRANSIT=20
Automatic Coding System</I> C28-4038, Copyright 1957, 1959 by IBM):</P>
<BLOCKQUOTE>
  <P>The FORTRAN system was designed for a more complex machine than the =
650,=20
  and consequently some of the 32 statements found in the FORTRAN =
Programmer's=20
  Reference Manual are not acceptable to the FOR TRANSIT system. In =
addition,=20
  certain restrictions to the FORTRAN language have been added. However, =
none of=20
  these restrictions make a source program written for FOR TRANSIT =
incompatible=20
  with the FORTRAN system for the 704.</P></BLOCKQUOTE>
<P>The permissible statements were:</P>
<DL>
  <DD>Arithmetic assignment statements, e.g., <CODE><CODE =
class=3D"mw-highlight"=20
  dir=3D"ltr">a =3D b</CODE></CODE></DD>
  <DD><CODE><CODE class=3D"mw-highlight" dir=3D"ltr">GO to =
n</CODE></CODE></DD>
  <DD><CODE>GO TO (n<SUB>1</SUB>, n<SUB>2</SUB>, ..., n<SUB>m</SUB>),=20
  i</CODE></DD>
  <DD><CODE>IF (a) n<SUB>1</SUB>, n<SUB>2</SUB>, =
n<SUB>3</SUB></CODE></DD>
  <DD><CODE><CODE class=3D"mw-highlight" =
dir=3D"ltr">PAUSE</CODE></CODE></DD>
  <DD><CODE><CODE class=3D"mw-highlight" =
dir=3D"ltr">STOP</CODE></CODE></DD>
  <DD><CODE>DO n i =3D m1, m2</CODE></DD>
  <DD><CODE><CODE class=3D"mw-highlight" =
dir=3D"ltr">CONTINUE</CODE></CODE></DD>
  <DD><CODE><CODE class=3D"mw-highlight" =
dir=3D"ltr">END</CODE></CODE></DD>
  <DD><CODE><CODE class=3D"mw-highlight" dir=3D"ltr">READ n, =
list</CODE></CODE></DD>
  <DD><CODE><CODE class=3D"mw-highlight" dir=3D"ltr">PUNCH n,=20
list</CODE></CODE></DD>
  <DD><CODE><CODE class=3D"mw-highlight" dir=3D"ltr">DIMENSION V, V, V,=20
  ...</CODE></CODE></DD>
  <DD><CODE><CODE class=3D"mw-highlight" dir=3D"ltr">EQUIVALENCE =
(a,b,c), (d,c),=20
  ...</CODE></CODE></DD></DL>
<P>Up to ten subroutines could be used in one program.</P>
<P>FOR TRANSIT statements were limited to columns 7 through 56, only. =
Punched=20
cards were used for input and output on the IBM 650. Three passes were =
required=20
to translate source code to the "IT" language, then to compile the IT =
statements=20
into SOAP assembly language, and finally to produce the object program, =
which=20
could then be loaded into the machine to run the program (using punched =
cards=20
for data input, and outputting results onto punched cards).</P>
<P>Two versions existed for the 650s with a 2000 word memory drum: FOR =
TRANSIT I=20
(S) and FOR TRANSIT II, the latter for machines equipped with indexing =
registers=20
and automatic floating point decimal (<A title=3D"Bi-quinary coded =
decimal" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bi-quinary_coded_decimal">bi-quinar=
y</A>)=20
arithmetic. Appendix A of the manual included wiring diagrams for the <A =
title=3D"IBM 533"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IBM_533">IBM 533</A> card =
reader/punch <A=20
title=3D"Plugboard" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plugboard">control=20
panel</A>.</P>
<H3><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" =
id=3D"Fortran-based_languages">Fortran-based=20
languages</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN =
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A=20
title=3D"Edit section: Fortran-based languages" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran&amp;action=3D=
edit&amp;section=3D31">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H3>
<P>Prior to FORTRAN 77, a number of <A title=3D"Preprocessor" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Preprocessor">preprocessors</A>=20
were commonly used to provide a friendlier language, with the advantage =
that the=20
preprocessed code could be compiled on any machine with a standard =
FORTRAN=20
compiler. These preprocesors would typically support <A =
title=3D"Structured programming"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Structured_programming">structured =

programming</A>, variable names longer than six characters, additional =
data=20
types, <A title=3D"Conditional compilation" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conditional_compilation">conditiona=
l=20
compilation</A>, and even <A title=3D"Macro (computer science)" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Macro_(computer_science)">macro</A>=
=20
capabilities. Popular preprocessors included <A title=3D"FLECS (page =
does not exist)"=20
class=3D"new" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFLECS&amp;action=3De=
dit&amp;redlink=3D1">FLECS</A>,=20
<A title=3D"Iftran (page does not exist)" class=3D"new" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DIftran&amp;action=3D=
edit&amp;redlink=3D1">iftran</A>,=20
<A title=3D"Mortran" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mortran">MORTRAN</A>, <A=20
title=3D"SFtran (page does not exist)" class=3D"new" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DSFtran&amp;action=3D=
edit&amp;redlink=3D1">SFtran</A>,=20
<A title=3D"S-Fortran (page does not exist)" class=3D"new" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DS-Fortran&amp;action=
=3Dedit&amp;redlink=3D1">S-Fortran</A>,=20
<A title=3D"Ratfor" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ratfor">Ratfor</A>, and <A=20
title=3D"Ratfiv" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ratfiv">Ratfiv</A>. Ratfor=20
and Ratfiv, for example, implemented a <A title=3D"C (programming =
language)" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C_(programming_language)">C</A>-lik=
e=20
language, outputting preprocessed code in standard FORTRAN 66. Despite =
advances=20
in the Fortran language, preprocessors continue to be used for =
conditional=20
compilation and macro substitution.</P>
<P>One of the earliest versions of FORTRAN, introduced in the 60's, was=20
popularly used in colleges and universities. Developed, supported, and=20
distributed by the <A title=3D"University of Waterloo" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_Waterloo">University =

of Waterloo</A>, <A title=3D"WATFIV"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/WATFIV">WATFOR</A> was based =
largely on=20
FORTRAN IV. A WATFOR student could submit their batch FORTRAN job and, =
if there=20
were no syntax errors, the program would move straight to execution. =
This=20
simplification allowed students to concentrate on their program's syntax =
and=20
semantics, or execution logic flow, rather than dealing with submission =
<A=20
title=3D"Job Control Language" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Job_Control_Language">Job=20
Control Language</A> (JCL), the compile/link-edit/execution successive=20
process(es), or other complexities of the mainframe/minicomputer =
environment. A=20
down side to this simplified environment was that WATFOR was not a good =
choice=20
for programmers needing the expanded abilities of their host =
processor(s), e.g.,=20
WATFOR typically had very limited access to I/O devices. WATFOR was =
succeeded by=20
<A title=3D"WATFIV" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/WATFIV">WATFIV</A> and its=20
later versions.</P>
<P>program; s=3D0 i=3D1,n; s=3Ds+1; stop i; s=3D's' Stop (line =
programing)</P>
<P><A title=3D"LRLTRAN (page does not exist)" class=3D"new" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DLRLTRAN&amp;action=3D=
edit&amp;redlink=3D1">LRLTRAN</A>=20
was developed at the <A title=3D"Lawrence Radiation Laboratory" =
class=3D"mw-redirect"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lawrence_Radiation_Laboratory">Lawr=
ence=20
Radiation Laboratory</A> to provide support for vector arithmetic and =
dynamic=20
storage, among other extensions to support systems programming. The =
distribution=20
included the <A title=3D"LTSS operating system (page does not exist)" =
class=3D"new"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DLTSS_operating_syste=
m&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;redlink=3D1">LTSS=20
operating system</A>.</P>
<P>The Fortran-95 Standard includes an optional <I>Part 3</I> which =
defines an=20
optional <A title=3D"Conditional compilation" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conditional_compilation">conditiona=
l=20
compilation</A> capability. This capability is often referred to as =
"CoCo".</P>
<P>Many Fortran compilers have integrated subsets of the <A title=3D"C =
preprocessor"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C_preprocessor">C preprocessor</A> =
into=20
their systems.</P>
<P><A title=3D"SIMSCRIPT"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SIMSCRIPT">SIMSCRIPT</A> is an =
application=20
specific Fortran preprocessor for modeling and simulating large discrete =

systems.</P>
<P>The <A title=3D"F (programming language)" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F_(programming_language)">F=20
programming language</A> was designed to be a clean subset of Fortran 95 =
that=20
attempted to remove the redundant, unstructured, and deprecated features =
of=20
Fortran, such as the <CODE>EQUIVALENCE</CODE> statement. F retains the =
array=20
features added in Fortran 90, and removes control statements that were =
made=20
obsolete by structured programming constructs added to both Fortran 77 =
and=20
Fortran 90. F is described by its creators as "a compiled, structured, =
array=20
programming language especially well suited to education and scientific=20
computing."<SUP class=3D"reference" id=3D"cite_ref-34"><A =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran#cite_note-34"><SPAN>[</SPAN=
>34<SPAN>]</SPAN></A></SUP></P>
<P>Lahey and Fujitsu teamed up to create Fortran for the Microsoft <A =
title=3D".NET Framework"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/.NET_Framework">.NET =
Framework</A>.<SUP=20
class=3D"reference" id=3D"cite_ref-35"><A =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran#cite_note-35"><SPAN>[</SPAN=
>35<SPAN>]</SPAN></A></SUP>=20
Silverfrost FTN95 is also capable of creating .NET code.<SUP =
class=3D"reference"=20
id=3D"cite_ref-36"><A=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran#cite_note-36"><SPAN>[</SPAN=
>36<SPAN>]</SPAN></A></SUP></P>
<H2><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" id=3D"Code_examples">Code =
examples</SPAN><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN =
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A title=3D"Edit section: Code =
examples"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran&amp;action=3D=
edit&amp;section=3D32">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H2>
<DIV class=3D"hatnote">For more details on this topic, see <A =
title=3D"wikibooks:Fortran/Fortran examples"=20
class=3D"extiw"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikibooks.org/wiki/Fortran/Fortran_examples">Wikibooks=
:Fortran/Fortran=20
examples</A>.</DIV>
<P>The following program illustrates dynamic memory allocation and =
array-based=20
operations, two features introduced with Fortran 90. Particularly =
noteworthy is=20
the absence of <CODE>DO</CODE> loops and =
<CODE>IF</CODE>/<CODE>THEN</CODE>=20
statements in manipulating the array; mathematical operations are =
applied to the=20
array as a whole. Also apparent is the use of descriptive variable names =
and=20
general code formatting that conform with contemporary programming =
style. This=20
example computes an average over data entered interactively.</P>
<DIV class=3D"mw-highlight mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"ltr">
